      <h1 class="article__title">
        We Asked Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini These Four Schengen Questions.
        Here's How They Did.
      </h1>

      <p class="article__meta">[Date] · [Read time] min read</p>

      <p class="article__audience">
        <strong>This post is for you if:</strong> you've ever typed your
        travel dates into an AI chatbot instead of a calculator, or you're
        wondering whether it's safe to.
        <br />
        <br />
        <strong>It's probably not necessary if:</strong> you already know
        never to trust an LLM with date arithmetic and just want a
        calculator — in that case, <a href="/app">skip straight to the
        tool</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article__body">

      <p>
        You're planning a return trip to Europe. You already have a rough
        sense of your dates, so instead of opening a calculator, you just
        ask whatever chatbot is already open in another tab: "Can I fly
        back into the Schengen Area on this date and stay for three
        weeks?" It answers instantly, confidently, in a full sentence. It
        sounds right. It also might be wrong — and unlike a calculator that
        shows its work, there's nothing to check it against except your own
        arithmetic, which is exactly what you were trying to avoid doing in
        the first place.
      </p>

      <p>
        We wanted to know how wrong, how often, and in which specific ways.
        So we took the worked scenarios from
        <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window">our rolling-window
        explainer</a> — real dates, real people, some of them intentionally
        tricky — and put the same four questions to the free versions of
        Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini, the ones you get without a
        subscription. This post is the methodology, the exact
        prompts (copy-pasteable, so you can reproduce or extend this
        yourself), and the results grid for each question.
      </p>

      <nav class="article__toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <p class="article__toc-title">On this page</p>
        <ol>
