      <h2 id="why-this-happens">Why chatbots struggle with this</h2>

      <p>
        None of this is a knock on any specific model. It's structural.
        Unless a chatbot decides to run code, it generates the next
        plausible token — it doesn't run arithmetic on a date object the
        way a calculator does.
        Adding and subtracting days across month boundaries, leap years,
        and a <em>rolling</em> 180-day lookback window (not a fixed period —
        see <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window">our explainer
        on that</a>) is exactly the kind of multi-step, exact, cumulative
        counting task language models are least reliable at, even when the
        prose surrounding the answer sounds completely authoritative. A
        wrong date delivered with full confidence is arguably worse than no
        answer at all, because it doesn't invite you to double check it.
      </p>

