      <!-- ================= QUESTION 4 ================= -->
      <h2 id="q4">Question 4 — "Can [Person] re-enter on [D] for [T] days?"</h2>

      <p>
        This is the yes/no version — the question someone asks right
        before clicking "book" on a flight. It's the most common real-world
        phrasing, and it's the one where a model can fail silently: a
        wrong "yes" looks identical to a right one until you're standing
        at the border. We've deliberately included two rows here that are
        designed to trip up a model that's just pattern-matching
        ("90 days, sounds fine") rather than actually computing the
        window.
      </p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Patricia, Aug 28 for 90 days (Scenario A)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Patricia holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Her entire Schengen Area travel history is a single trip: she entered on March 1, 2025 and exited on May 29, 2025. No other history.

Can Patricia enter the Schengen Area on August 28, 2025 and stay for a full 90 days without overstaying the 90/180 rule at any point? Answer yes or no first, then briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Published answer: Yes — <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#permanent-90th-day">see the "permanent 90th day" discussion</a>. This is the tightest possible legal case — no buffer at all.</p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Patricia, Aug 20 for 90 days (Scenario A, trap)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Patricia holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Her entire Schengen Area travel history is a single trip: she entered on March 1, 2025 and exited on May 29, 2025. No other history.

Can Patricia enter the Schengen Area on August 20, 2025 and stay for a full 90 days without overstaying the 90/180 rule at any point? Answer yes or no first, then briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Eight days too early — this should come back <strong>no</strong>. A model that got the Aug 28 case right by rote (rather than by actually computing the window) may still get this one wrong.</p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Ilker, Aug 1 for 90 days (Scenario B)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Ilker holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. His only prior Schengen Area travel: entered February 1, 2025, exited March 22, 2025 (50 days). No other history.

Can Ilker enter the Schengen Area on August 1, 2025 and stay for a full 90 days without overstaying the 90/180 rule at any point? Answer yes or no first, then briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Published answer: Yes — <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-3">see Ilker's worked example</a>.</p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Anna, May 3 for 50 days (Scenario B, trap)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Anna holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Her only prior Schengen Area travel: entered February 1, 2025, exited March 22, 2025 (50 days). No other history.

Can Anna enter the Schengen Area on May 3, 2025 and stay for 50 days without overstaying the 90/180 rule at any point? Answer yes or no first, then briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">This should come back <strong>no</strong> — Anna is capped at 40 days on that date (<a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-3">see the worked example</a>), ten short of the 50 asked about. A very plausible real mistake, since 40 and 50 both round to "about a month and a half."</p>

